---
// /marks/chinese/works-of-art: marks and makers on cloisonné and enamels, bronze, jade, snuff
// bottles and lacquer.
import MarkSection from '../../../components/ref/MarkSection.astro';
import { WORKS_OF_ART_GROUPS } from '../../../lib/records';
---
<MarkSection path="/marks/chinese/works-of-art" prefix="cnwoa" groups={WORKS_OF_ART_GROUPS} date="2026-11-27"
  title="Marks on Chinese Cloisonné, Bronze, Jade, Snuff Bottles and Lacquer | Heirloom"
  description="{n} marks and makers on Chinese cloisonné, bronze, jade, snuff bottles and lacquer, from Jingtai and Xuande marks to Lao Tian Li and the inside painters, with what each says about date."
  h1="Marks on Chinese works of art"
  lede="Cloisonné, bronze, jade, snuff bottles and lacquer carry their own marks: reign marks borrowed from porcelain, workshop names, and the signatures of the painters of snuff bottles. Each entry says what the mark is and what it does and does not say about date."
  placeholder="Characters or a name: 景泰, 宣德, 老天利, Ye Zhongsan" noun={['entry', 'entries']}
  app="Photograph the mark or signature. The Advisor reads it and finds it in this same reference.">
  <p>On these materials more than on porcelain, a reign mark is usually a tribute: Jingtai on cloisonné and Xuande on bronze appear mostly on pieces made long after those reigns. Workshop names and signed, dated snuff bottles are better evidence, and are copied too.</p>
</MarkSection>
